      "id": "ES-C17-futuro",
      "sequence": 1170,
      "title": "hablaré — keep the infinitive, add the future",
      "headword": "hablaré",
      "romanization": "hablaré",
      "gloss": "the singular simple future of hablar — hablaré, hablarás, hablará",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:702b9521339d627c",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Voy a hablar español uses the near-future frame you already know. Spanish also has a one-word future: Hablaré español — “I will speak Spanish.” It can present a future prediction or intention without adding a new subject or object."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: three familiar persons",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Keep the whole infinitive hablar, then add one ending:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "person",
                "simple future"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "utterances": [
                "person: yo. simple future: hablaré.",
                "person: tú. simple future: hablarás.",
                "person: él / ella / usted. simple future: hablará."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say hablaré, hablarás, hablará. Each form has final stress, marked by an accent. Plural persons wait."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The weld, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Late Latin and early Romance used more than one way to speak about the future. One influential construction placed an infinitive beside present forms of Latin habēre, “to have.” Through repeated use and sound change, descendants of those pieces became the endings in forms such as hablaré."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That history explains why the infinitive remains visible. It does not mean that a modern learner should attach the full modern verb haber, and it does not require the claim that every older future vanished at one moment."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
